-- avg over a single-histogram group: the sample itself
[]	1970-01-01 00:01:45.000	\N	(0,0,0,4,10,0,[(0,2)],[1,3],[],[],[])
-- Kahan accumulation e2e: the naive sum loses the two ones (ulp(1e16) = 2), the Kahan sum
-- keeps them: sum = 1e16+2 = 10000000000000002, avg = 3333333333333334 (naive ...3333.5)
[]	1970-01-01 00:01:45.000	\N	(0,0,0,3,10000000000000002,0,[(0,1)],[10000000000000002],[],[],[])
[]	1970-01-01 00:01:45.000	\N	(0,0,0,1,3333333333333334,0,[(0,1)],[3333333333333334],[],[],[])
-- overflow: the `sum` of the two 1.5e308 histograms overflows to +Inf (upstream keeps it),
-- while `avg` switches to the incremental mean and stays finite (1.5e308)
[]	1970-01-01 00:01:45.000	\N	(0,0,0,inf,inf,0,[(0,1)],[inf],[],[],[])
[]	1970-01-01 00:01:45.000	\N	(0,0,0,1.5e308,1.5e308,0,[(0,1)],[1.5e308],[],[],[])
-- a group whose only series has no sample in the lookback window produces nothing
-- the float arm of a mixed-kind series is masked per step: mx_h alone sums to the histogram
-- itself, while mx_f and mx_h together (one group mixing both kinds at the step) drop it
[]	1970-01-01 00:01:45.000	\N	(0,0,0,4,10,0,[(0,2)],[1,3],[],[],[])
